- CakePHP Version: 3.3.0
- Platform and Target: Apache 2.4.12, PHP 5.6.11, MySQL 5.6.25
### What you did

Converted my application to the new PSR7 middleware stack by replacing my webroot/index.php, config/bootstrap.php and added src/Application.php (also bin/cake.php for the vendor autoload) Everything was taken from cakephp/app 3.3.0 and then I fine tunned the new bootstrap with my own settings, plugins, whatever.

Also worth mentioning is that I store my sessions in mysql (using the schema/sessions.sql code)

Anyway, everything seemed to work except for the login. I'm using Cookies for a "remember me" feature and when submitting the form it wouldn't log me in at all, it would just redirect me back to the login page like a refresh, no error message, nothing.

What actually happened was that a new CAKEPHP id was generated that was not logged in and the old one was actually updated and logged in, however my browser used the new regenerated id. With a Chrome addon called EditThisCookie, when editing my CAKEPHP session id with the old one that was before i was actually logged in (because it contained the Auth session key that the new session id didn't)

I don't know how to explain this better but it looks like if you're trying to setup a cookie after successfully logging in, cake will regenerate a new session id and use that instead of using the one that it had which was updated with the Auth key holding my user info.

Now code examples:
AppController

```
class AppController extends Controller
{
    // load components
    public function initialize() {
        parent::initialize();

        $this->loadComponent('RequestHandler');
        $this->loadComponent('Flash');
        $this->loadComponent('Cookie');
        $this->loadComponent('Auth', [
                'authenticate' => [
                        'Form' => [
                                'fields' => ['username' => 'email', 'password' => 'passwd']
                        ]
                ]
        ]);
...
```

Login action in a controller

```
    public function login() {
        // on post make the login
        if($this->request->is('post')) {
            // identify
            $user = $this->Auth->identify();
            if($user) {
                // login
                $this->Auth->setUser($user);
                $this->Flash->success('You have successfully logged in');

                // THIS IS THE CODE THAT BREAKS THE LOGIN
                $this->Cookie->configKey('RememberMe', ['expires' => '+1 month']);
                $this->Cookie->write('RememberMe', $user);

                // redirect when finished
                return $this->redirect($this->Auth->redirectUrl());

            // user could not be identified
            } else $this->Flash->error('Wrong username or password');
        }
    }
```

And then I have an autologin code in beforeFilter that basically checks the cookie and performs the login automatically which isn't important to show here because I never actually reach that point.
### Expected Behavior

The login to work, cookie to be written, the Auth key to exist in the current session
### Actual Behavior

Cookie is indeed written but the CAKEPHP id is renewed so the Auth session key doesn't exists in the current session and the login fails.

If I comment out the cookie code from `// THIS IS THE CODE THAT BREAKS THE LOGIN` the CAKEPHP session is also renewed but replaced and it contains the Auth session key needed.

The old session id is also deleted from the database where as when setting a cookie it is not deleted but merely left there and the sessions table gets filled up quickly. The table reached 50+ records from me alone trying to debug the damned thing, so there's clearly a problem somewhere.
### Conclusion

I've since switched back to the Dispatch Filters that are now deprecated but at least my application works as it should.
